    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b3b8feb6-564e-51dd-9828-a34a21277258",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Evaluate ${\\int}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\text{d}\\mathbf{r}$ given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(- 2 y)\\,\\mathbf{i}+(- 2 x)\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)=(2 t)\\,\\mathbf{i}+(3 t^{2})\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in[1,2].$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=\\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot \\mathbf{r}^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2 t,\\quad y=3 t^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(- 2 y)\\,\\mathbf{i}+(- 2 x)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\left(- 2 \\left(3 t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(- 2 \\left(2 t\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(- 6 t^{2})\\,\\mathbf{i}+(- 4 t)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 t^{2})\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(2)\\,\\mathbf{i}+(6 t)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot \\mathbf{r}^{\\prime}(t) \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left({(- 6 t^{2})\\,\\mathbf{i}+(- 4 t)\\,\\mathbf{j}}\\right) \\\\ &{} \\cdot\\left({(2)\\,\\mathbf{i}+(6 t)\\,\\mathbf{j}}\\right)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=(- 6 t^{2})\\cdot(2)+(- 4 t)\\cdot(6 t) \\\\ &=\\left({- 12 t^{2}}\\right)+\\left({- 24 t^{2}}\\right) \\\\ &=- 36 t^{2}\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\int_C \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\ \\begin{aligned} &=\\int_{1}^{2}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot \\mathbf{r}^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left({- 36 t^{2}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[{- 12 t^{3}}\\right]_{1}^{2} \\\\ &=((-96))-((-12)) \\\\ &=(-96)-(-12) \\\\ &=-84\\end{aligned}\\end{gathered}\n$$",
